   [#191](https://github.com/SethMMorton/natsort/pull/191))
 - Eliminated mypy failure related to string literals
+- The source distribution shipped whatever development state happened to be
+  tracked in git, because there was no `MANIFEST.in` to prune the file list
+  setuptools-scm supplies; it now excludes tooling state while still carrying
+  the package, the tests and the packaging files
+- `os_sorted` and `os_sort_keygen` document that every input is coerced to
+  `str` before collating, but on a POSIX host without PyICU they did not, so
+  the same non-string input sorted differently depending on whether PyICU was
+  installed; all three branches now share one coercion step
+- The CLI printed one blank line when stdin held nothing, because splitting an
+  empty string yields a single empty entry; empty input is now no entries at
+  all, in both newline and NUL separator modes
+- The installed `natsort` console script printed a full traceback for a
+  mistyped `--filter` range or a Ctrl-C, because the handlers lived in the
+  `python -m natsort` path only; both entry points now go through a shared
+  `cli` function and report one line
+- The PyICU sentinel that `ns.NUMAFTER` places before numbers was the three
+  ASCII characters `x7f` rather than the maximum byte, so with PyICU installed
+  numbers were sorted before, not after, any string whose collation key began
+  above byte `0x78`
 
 ### Removed
 
